\description{
This function orchestrates the demographic herd module
\code{\link[=run_demographic_herd_module]{run_demographic_herd_module()}} and the non-demographic herd module
\code{\link[=run_nondemographic_herd_module]{run_nondemographic_herd_module()}} within a single wrapper. Depending on the
selected logical switches, it runs the demographic module, the
non-demographic module, or both, and returns a harmonized herd-module output
structure for downstream use in \code{\link[=run_gleam]{run_gleam()}}.
}
\details{
The function supports three execution modes:
\enumerate{
\item Demographic only: see \code{\link[=run_demographic_herd_module]{run_demographic_herd_module()}} for additional details.
\item Non-demographic only: see \code{\link[=run_nondemographic_herd_module]{run_nondemographic_herd_module()}} for additional details.
\item Demographic and non-demographic together:
When \code{run_demographic = TRUE} and \code{run_nondemographic = TRUE}, the function:
\enumerate{
\item runs \code{\link[=run_demographic_herd_module]{run_demographic_herd_module()}} on the demographic subset
\item derives annual non-demographic entrants from the demographic output
\item runs \code{\link[=run_nondemographic_herd_module]{run_nondemographic_herd_module()}} on the non-demographic subset
\item row-binds the two cohort-level result tables
\item harmonizes shared output columns
\item rescales average stock and offtake to the herd total
}
}

When both modules are run, annual entrants into the non-demographic blocks
are derived from the demographic output:
\itemize{
\item female entrants to \code{FN} are taken from demographic cohort \code{FJ} as
\code{cohort_stock_fem_annual_nondemo = cohort_stock_annual_nondemographic}
\item male entrants to \code{MN} are taken from demographic cohort \code{MJ} as
\code{cohort_stock_mal_annual_nondemo = cohort_stock_annual_nondemographic}
}

These entrant counts are merged with herd-level non-demographic timing inputs
such as:
\itemize{
\item \code{rest_between_nondemo_cycles_duration}
\item \code{phase1_nondemo_fem_duration_days}
\item \code{phase2_nondemo_fem_duration_days}
\item \code{phase1_nondemo_mal_duration_days}
\item \code{phase2_nondemo_mal_duration_days}
}

After the selected modules run, the function combines their cohort-level
outputs using \code{data.table::rbindlist(..., use.names = TRUE, fill = TRUE)}.
This allows demographic and non-demographic rows to share a common schema.

The combined cohort output harmonizes, when needed:
\itemize{
\item \code{offtake_heads}
\item \code{offtake_heads_assessment}
\item \code{cohort_duration_days}
\item \code{death_rate}
\item \code{cohort_stock_size}
}

When both modules are run, intermediate unscaled cohort stock and offtake
outputs are combined and rescaled so that the summed cohort average stock
matches \code{herd_size_total} from \code{herd_level_data}. The same rescaling applies
to non-demographic-only runs when \code{herd_size_total} is supplied.

The rescaling is performed in two steps:
\enumerate{
\item \code{cohort_stock_size_unscaled} is rescaled internally to
\code{cohort_stock_size} within each \code{herd_id}
\item \code{offtake_heads_unscaled} and
\code{offtake_heads_assessment_unscaled} are rescaled proportionally to the
final \code{cohort_stock_size}
}

When only the demographic module is run, its scaled outputs are renamed to
the harmonized final output names. Non-demographic-only runs without
\code{herd_size_total} return unscaled stock and offtake under those names.

The function then rescales the combined cohort
stock and offtake outputs so that the summed cohort average stock matches
\code{herd_size_total} from \code{herd_level_data}.

The rescaling is performed in two steps:
\enumerate{
\item \code{cohort_stock_size_unscaled} is rescaled to \code{cohort_stock_size}
within each \code{herd_id}
\item \code{offtake_heads} and \code{offtake_heads_assessment} are rescaled
proportionally from the unscaled cohort stock to the scaled cohort stock
}

Without a herd-size target in non-demographic-only mode,
\code{cohort_stock_size_unscaled} is simply renamed to \code{cohort_stock_size}.
}
\section{Use cases}{

\subsection{Use case 1: Run both demographic and non-demographic modules}{

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# Load herd simulation inputs (cohort and herd-level)
herd_all_chrt_dt <- data.table::fread(system.file(
  "extdata/run_modules_examples/herd_all_input_chrt_data.csv",
  package = "gleam"
))
herd_all_hrd_dt <- data.table::fread(system.file(
  "extdata/run_modules_examples/herd_all_input_hrd_data.csv",
  package = "gleam"
))

# Run mixed demographic and non-demographic herd simulation
results <- run_all_herd_module(
  cohort_level_data = herd_all_chrt_dt,
  herd_level_data = herd_all_hrd_dt,
  run_demographic = TRUE,
  run_nondemographic = TRUE,
  simulation_duration = 365,
  show_indicator = TRUE
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

